WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:07.600
This is a case study from a real MSHA accident

00:07.600 --> 00:11.338
report. When a miner is killed, MSHA investigates

00:11.338 --> 00:13.862
and publishes what happened, the root causes it

00:13.862 --> 00:16.688
found, and what the mine changed afterward.

00:16.688 --> 00:19.388
We don't use these reports to blame anyone.

00:19.388 --> 00:21.788
We use them because the same rock and the same

00:21.788 --> 00:24.163
tasks are part of our work, and the lessons were

00:24.163 --> 00:26.087
paid for at a terrible price.

00:27.204 --> 00:29.679
This one happened at an underground limestone mine

00:29.679 --> 00:33.042
in Missouri, a room-and-pillar mine much like ours.

00:33.042 --> 00:36.304
The report is titled Fatal Fall of Rib Accident,

00:36.304 --> 00:39.779
and the date is April 11, 2014.

00:40.902 --> 00:43.539
MSHA reports follow the same layout.

00:43.539 --> 00:46.352
An overview tells you what happened in a few lines.

00:46.352 --> 00:48.239
The description walks through the shift,

00:48.239 --> 00:51.739
step by step. Then comes the root cause analysis.

00:51.739 --> 00:54.815
A root cause is a basic reason the accident happened

00:54.815 --> 00:57.477
that, if it had been controlled, would have prevented

00:57.477 --> 01:01.590
it. The report then lists the citations MSHA issued

01:01.590 --> 01:03.789
and what the mine did to fix the problem.

01:04.920 --> 01:07.757
As you watch, ask yourself two questions.

01:07.757 --> 01:09.970
What was the first control that was missing?

01:09.970 --> 01:11.907
And what would I have done in that spot?

01:11.907 --> 01:14.395
You'll answer those same questions in the activity

01:14.395 --> 01:15.632
after this video.

01:18.958 --> 01:21.408
Two miners were scaling the rib of a pillar by

01:21.408 --> 01:24.033
hand. They were working from a man basket on the

01:24.033 --> 01:27.233
boom of a truck, high up the rib. The report puts

01:27.233 --> 01:30.146
the basket about thirty-eight feet above the floor.

01:30.146 --> 01:33.108
At this mine, scaling was the main way of controlling

01:33.108 --> 01:35.558
loose ground. They worked the rib with scaling

01:35.558 --> 01:37.909
bars, prying down loose rock.

01:39.028 --> 01:41.715
While they were scaling, a very large slab came

01:41.715 --> 01:44.465
off the rib. The report estimates it at around

01:44.465 --> 01:47.778
twenty-five tons. Part of it, several tons,

01:47.778 --> 01:50.328
struck an outrigger on the truck, and the boom

01:50.328 --> 01:52.865
and basket came down to the mine floor.

01:52.865 --> 01:54.191
Both miners died.

01:55.330 --> 01:57.580
They were experienced, and they were doing a job

01:57.580 --> 02:00.043
the mine did regularly. That's what makes this

02:00.043 --> 02:02.868
report hard to read. Nothing about the task looked

02:02.868 --> 02:06.268
unusual that day, right up until the rib let go.

02:07.396 --> 02:09.809
Think about where everything was. The miners were

02:09.809 --> 02:13.046
high on the rib, in a basket, with no way to step

02:13.046 --> 02:15.784
back. The truck holding the boom was parked where

02:15.784 --> 02:18.346
falling rock could reach it. So even though the

02:18.346 --> 02:21.272
rock didn't land in the basket, it hit the machine

02:21.272 --> 02:23.647
holding the basket up. When you put equipment in

02:23.647 --> 02:26.184
the fall zone, you put everyone who depends on

02:26.184 --> 02:28.272
that equipment in the fall zone too.

02:29.386 --> 02:31.886
Tall ribs and big pillars make it hard to judge

02:31.886 --> 02:34.511
how much rock is loose. From below,

02:34.511 --> 02:36.661
a crack can look like a small piece.

02:36.661 --> 02:40.011
Behind it, the slab can run deep into the pillar.

02:43.340 --> 02:46.677
MSHA's root cause was this: mine management did

02:46.677 --> 02:49.390
not ensure that scaling was done from a location

02:49.390 --> 02:52.502
that would not expose the miners to falling material.

02:52.502 --> 02:55.465
The basket put the miners and the equipment inside

02:55.465 --> 02:57.465
the zone where the rock could land.

02:58.611 --> 03:01.248
That's exactly what the federal standard covers.

03:01.248 --> 03:04.023
Where manual scaling is done, it has to be done

03:04.023 --> 03:06.848
from a location that won't expose people to injury

03:06.848 --> 03:09.410
from falling material, or other protection from

03:09.410 --> 03:11.673
falling material has to be provided.

03:12.812 --> 03:15.188
The report lists other problems too,

03:15.188 --> 03:17.975
which it says did not contribute to the accident.

03:17.975 --> 03:20.350
Loose ground in the area had been seen but was

03:20.350 --> 03:23.037
not barricaded or posted with warnings.

03:23.037 --> 03:25.337
And workplace exam records were missing for the

03:25.337 --> 03:28.125
week before. Those are both things you heard about

03:28.125 --> 03:30.800
in the workplace exam lesson. They matter because

03:30.800 --> 03:32.950
they're the early warnings that keep people out

03:32.950 --> 03:34.387
from under bad ground.

03:35.498 --> 03:37.910
After the accident, the mine wrote a procedure

03:37.910 --> 03:40.810
for setting up scaling equipment. It set a distance

03:40.810 --> 03:43.335
between the equipment and the rib, and it used

03:43.335 --> 03:46.198
a berm to keep the equipment back. The idea is

03:46.198 --> 03:49.173
simple. If the rock comes down, it lands where

03:49.173 --> 03:50.698
nobody is standing.

03:54.012 --> 03:56.424
Now look at our own record. At the Clover Bottom

03:56.424 --> 03:59.799
old mine, MSHA's public records show four separate

03:59.799 --> 04:05.874
times, in 2005, 2008, 2011 and 2014,

04:05.874 --> 04:09.112
that rock fell from the face or the back onto miners.

04:09.112 --> 04:11.974
They were hand-scaling, measuring a freshly scaled

04:11.974 --> 04:14.699
room, checking for loose rock, or preparing to

04:14.699 --> 04:18.425
load a shot. In 2014, the rock above the piece

04:18.425 --> 04:20.099
being pulled came down.

04:21.222 --> 04:24.447
In 2021 at Clover Bottom, rock slid off a lower

04:24.447 --> 04:27.260
rib and pinched a drill's dust hose.

04:27.260 --> 04:30.235
The driller reached in to move it, the rock shifted,

04:30.235 --> 04:32.935
and his elbow struck the rib. And at Boonesboro

04:32.935 --> 04:36.672
in 2020, a scaler lost a tooth, the operator walked

04:36.672 --> 04:39.209
toward the face to pick it up, and a rock fell

04:39.209 --> 04:40.334
on his shoulder.

04:41.461 --> 04:44.161
The pattern is the same as the Missouri report.

04:44.161 --> 04:46.648
Rock that looks fine can let go when the rock below

04:46.648 --> 04:49.661
it moves, or a slab can be much bigger than it

04:49.661 --> 04:51.998
looks from the floor. Every one of these happened

04:51.998 --> 04:55.361
during scaling, checking, or shot preparation.

04:55.361 --> 04:57.773
That's the moment when people are closest to ground

04:57.773 --> 04:59.435
that's just been disturbed.

05:00.546 --> 05:02.971
Our sites mine the same kind of rock in the same

05:02.971 --> 05:06.059
room-and-pillar layout. Boonesboro and Dix River

05:06.059 --> 05:08.471
both work the Camp Nelson Limestone,

05:08.471 --> 05:11.384
and Clover Bottom's old and new mines are room-and-pillar

05:11.384 --> 05:14.159
too. So the Missouri report isn't about somebody

05:14.159 --> 05:17.509
else's problem. It describes the same kind of ground,

05:17.509 --> 05:20.284
the same kind of task, and the same kind of moment

05:20.284 --> 05:22.046
our own records point to.

05:25.397 --> 05:27.635
Here's what this means for you. You scale only

05:27.635 --> 05:29.584
if you've been trained and authorized for that

05:29.584 --> 05:32.310
task and that equipment. If you are,

05:32.310 --> 05:34.310
you work from a protected position,

05:34.310 --> 05:37.447
under supported, examined ground, with your tool

05:37.447 --> 05:39.935
reaching the loose rock and your body well back

05:39.935 --> 05:42.709
from where it can land. You never stand under or

05:42.709 --> 05:45.072
beside the rock you're pulling, or under the rock

05:45.072 --> 05:45.934
above it.

05:47.062 --> 05:50.137
After scaling, the area is examined again before

05:50.137 --> 05:53.774
anyone moves in to drill or load. Our sites' scaling

05:53.774 --> 05:56.062
methods and equipment are covered in your task

05:56.062 --> 05:56.837
training.

05:57.952 --> 05:59.964
If you're not the scaler, stay out.

05:59.964 --> 06:03.077
Don't walk up to the face to grab a tool or a part.

06:03.077 --> 06:05.777
Don't reach in by hand to free a hose or a cable

06:05.777 --> 06:08.377
next to a rib. If you see loose ground,

06:08.377 --> 06:12.214
cracks, or a fresh slab, back out to examined ground,

06:12.214 --> 06:15.514
keep others out, and report it. If it's not barricaded,

06:15.514 --> 06:17.864
it should be. Tell your supervisor.

06:19.007 --> 06:22.169
Before any work near a rib, face or back,

06:22.169 --> 06:24.819
ask five questions. Where will the rock go if it

06:24.819 --> 06:28.044
lets go? Where am I standing? Where is my machine?

06:28.044 --> 06:30.007
What's above the rock I'm working on?

06:30.007 --> 06:32.369
And who knows I'm here? If you can't answer all

06:32.369 --> 06:35.269
five, or if the answer to the first one is "on

06:35.269 --> 06:37.944
me," stop and talk to your supervisor before you

06:37.944 --> 06:38.744
start.

06:39.881 --> 06:42.556
That goes for every job close to the ground,

06:42.556 --> 06:45.731
not just scaling. Drilling, loading a round,

06:45.731 --> 06:48.468
measuring, and cleaning up near a rib all put you

06:48.468 --> 06:50.843
close to rock that may have been disturbed.

06:51.983 --> 06:54.383
Four things to remember. Scaling is done from a

06:54.383 --> 06:57.046
place where falling rock can't reach you.

06:57.046 --> 06:59.495
The rock above can come with the rock you're pulling.

06:59.495 --> 07:01.933
Loose ground gets posted and barricaded until it's

07:01.933 --> 07:05.021
fixed. And the area gets examined again before

07:05.021 --> 07:07.771
anyone goes back in. If something about the ground

07:07.771 --> 07:10.908
doesn't look or sound right, stop and report it.

07:12.040 --> 07:15.202
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

07:15.202 --> 07:16.702
override this video.
